  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Seychelles Chamber of Commerce and Industry (SCCI) launched a policy framework on &#x201c;Pathways to Recovery &amp; Prosperity (RAP)&#x201d; in July 2020. The RAP, accompanied by an advocacy strategy, was developed through an analysis of the business environment, surveys and stakeholder interviews; and systematic/continuous engagement with stakeholders. In July 2020, SCCI presented RAP to the national President and Senior Ministers, and attracted headline news in print media and television. The President actioned the RAP to be implemented in collaboration with Ministries. SCCI was invited to participate in weekly high-level dialogue chaired by the President. The RAP framework provided a set of 7 priorities for SCCI who subsequently conducted five enterprise surveys (629 responses), organised 4 focus group meetings with members and other business associations, participated in 7 high-level meetings with senior policy makers and submitted 10 high-level policy papers/public statements. For example, in Feb 2021, SCCI published &quot;A Comprehensive Framework to Emerge Stronger in the New Reality&quot;, a response to the national budget announced by the Minister of Finance. In March 2021, SCCI published a proposal addressing redundancy, training and reskilling. The RAP positioned SCCI as the lead actor in driving the recovery agenda. For instance, SCCI partnered with Central Bank on reopening of the economy by providing technical guidance in 7 meetings and 2 workshops held in Aug/Sept 2020. SCCI also specifically followed-up to a policy priority from the RAP on entrepreneurship by establishing SIYB training in Dec 2020. To support the implementation of the RAP, SCCI introduced a Policy Communication Tracker tool, which provided members with an ongoing update of SCCI&#x2019;s policy priorities and engagements (media interventions, engagement with Ministries etc). The government incorporated the RAP as the main private sector development component into the National Development Strategy (Vision 2033). The ILO established a survey platform that enabled SCCI to run 7 enterprise surveys to identify members needs/concerns from May 2020 to July2021. The ILO provided SCCI with technical support for the development of the RAP policy framework by assisting the analysis of the survey data, policy formulation and messaging. In addition, the ILO provided 10 technical notes and analysis on key policy components of the RAP. To assist SCCI&#x2019;s advocacy and communication efforts for the RAP, the ILO assisted by developing media and communication strategy. The ILO also provided SCCI with two policy frameworks, one as the pandemic broke to help SCCI engage with government and the second at the start of 2021 to support and update the RAP framework. Specifically in this regard, the ILO provided SCCI with a policy framework on E-platforms and an ancillary research paper and also helped SCCI establish a SCCI working committee on employment law review, the first working committee of SCCI. The ILO provided SCCI with a range of COVID 19 material that was adapted into 5 guides, 2 training videos, webinars and speeches. The material was specifically customized for SCCI use, and this adaption was supported by the ILO. The ILO also supported and facilitated a leadership mentor programme for the Chairman of SCCI to develop policy advocacy strategies (4 coaching sessions with a top international advocacy expert). The ILO supported SCCI&#x2019;s new service on by training 10 SIYB trainers. The ILO also supported the development and launch of a &#x201c;Seychelles NEXT&#x201d; report on the changing nature of the workplace post pandemic (September 2021).</narrative>
   </description>
